:h1 res=2 id=Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment.Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment
.br
Software development normally involves managing a number of different files for each project&per.  A project often includes 
multiple source files, include files, and libraries&per.  In addition, there are usually several programming tools involved&colon. 
 compilers, linkers, preprocessors, debuggers, editors, and so on&per.  But this is likely not new to you&per.  What will 
be new is the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment (IDE)&per.
.br
.br
The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment is a software development tool that manages project files and 
language tools&per.  Although it is really a tool itself, the common term to describe it (and possibly the best analogy) 
is:hp2. Development Environment&per.:ehp2.  By using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, you have direct 
access to each of the files relating to a project and all of the language tools you require to build your application&per.
.br
.br
:artwork align=center name='ide_s.bmp'.

:h2 res=3 id=How_the_Environment_Operates.How the Environment Operates
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, environment
.br
The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment has a graphical interface that combines file management and tool management&per. 
 An IDE session deals with a:hp2. Project&per.:ehp2.  A project can be generally defined as the collection of files and necessary 
information that relates to the development of project components&per.  However, a project is more than just a graphical 
representation of a file list-it contains all of the underlying smarts to understand the interrelation of all files associated 
with the project and to ensure that these files are up-to-date&per.
.br
.br
Each project component is referred to as a:hp2. Target&per.:ehp2.  An Integrated Development Environment target is 
made up of the source files, include files, libraries, and MAKE instructions to create the target file (for example, "&per.exe", 
"&per.dll", "&per.lib", or "&per.hlp")&per.  A project often has more than one target&per.  Each target can be set to create 
the associated target file for a specific destination platform&per.
.br
.br
Within a target, there are:hp2. Sources&per.:ehp2.  A source is an individual file (such as a "&per.c" or "&per.cpp" 
file, but it can also be a "&per.bmp", "&per.rc", or "&per.lib" file) used to create the target file&per.  A target usually 
has more than one source&per.

:h2 res=4 id=How_the_Integrated_Development_Environment_Looks.How the Integrated Development Environment Looks
:i1.look and feel of the Integrated Development Environment
.br
In the following illustration, the main window is the project window for a project entitled:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
first&per.wpj&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  The sub-window represents the target for this project,:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
target1&per.exe&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  The source file for the target is listed within the target window&per. 
 In this project, there is only a single target with a single source file&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide1.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 1&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. The Open Watcom IDE window displays project and target windows&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The language tools are available on the toolbar and the drop-down menus&per.  For example, on the Sources menu, you 
can work with the source that is selected in the target window&per.  Here you can add a new source, remove or rename an existing 
source, edit a source, and so on&per.  A similar set of target actions are available in the Targets menu and project actions 
are located in the File menu&per.
.br
.br
A single IDE session displays a project&per.  If the project consists of a number of components, such as two executables 
and one library, these appear as target sub-windows on the project window&per.  Each target window displays the files necessary 
to construct the target and associates files with a rule describing the construction mechanism based on filename extension&per. 
 For example, a filename with the extension "&per.exe" may associate with the rule for constructing executables, or a filename 
with the extension "&per.lib" may associate with the rule for constructing static libraries&per.
.br
.br
Different projects can refer to the same target&per.  If they do, either project can manipulate the shared target 
and any changes to either project affect the shared target&per.

:h2 res=5 id=The_Files_the_Integrated_Development_Environment_Uses.The Files the Integrated Development Environment Uses
:i1.projects
.br
The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment creates and manages a project, referencing a number of targets&per. 
 The IDE uses filename extensions to represent projects and targets&per.  The extension "&per.wpj" represents projects and 
the extension "&per.tgt" represents targets&per.  The IDE compiles and links the target in the target directory&per.
.br
.br
The project file (&per.wpj) contains the names of all of the target files related to the project, including housekeeping 
information about the project and the current IDE session&per.
.br
.br
Each target file (&per.tgt) describes one target and includes the name and type of the target executable, a reference 
to a rule describing how to build the target, and a list of relevant switches the rule uses&per.  In addition, target files 
also contain the names of all of the source files used to construct the target program, references to similar rules for compiling 
them, and relevant switches&per.
.br
.br
In constructing targets, the Integrated Development Environment references a list of rules defined in a configuration 
file, called:font facename=Courier size=12x10. ide&per.cfg&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  Each rule defines a command 
line used to do the construction and a list of actions you can perform on the target program (such as make, run, or debug) 
or on the files in the item list (such as make or edit)&per.  Additionally, rules refer to tools&per.
.br
.br
Tools define which command line switches you can use in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment&per.

:h2 res=6 id=Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment.Using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, using the
.br
This section discusses the following topics&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Starting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:li.Quitting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:li.The IDE Menu Bar
:li.The IDE Status Bar
:li.The IDE Toolbar
:eul.

:h3 res=7 id=Starting_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment.Starting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, starting the
.br
To start the Integrated Development Environment, double-click on the IDE icon&per.  This opens the Open Watcom IDE Project 
window&per.

:h3 res=8 id=Quitting_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment.Quitting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, quitting the
.br
To exit the Integrated Development Environment, choose Exit from the File menu of the Open Watcom IDE Project window 
or double-click on the system menu box on the same window&per.

:h3 res=9 id=The_IDE_Menu_Bar.The IDE Menu Bar
:i1.menu bar, Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, menu bar
.br
The IDE's menu bar consists of the following eight menus&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.File:ehp2.
:dd.Open new or existing projects, close and save projects, exit the IDE, and show or hide the toolbar and status bar
:dt.:hp2.Actions:ehp2.
:dd.Make all targets, run applications, and invoke other GUI Tools
:dt.:hp2.Targets:ehp2.
:dd.Work with targets, including setting switches and creating, removing, and renaming targets
:dt.:hp2.Sources:ehp2.
:dd.Work with sources, including setting switches and creating, removing, and renaming sources
:dt.:hp2.Options:ehp2.
:dd.Set switches for creating targets and sources
:dt.:hp2.Log:ehp2.
:dd.Perform functions relating to the IDE Log window
:dt.:hp2.Window:ehp2.
:dd.Refresh the contents of the IDE windows and view the targets currently available on the IDE window
:dt.:hp2.Help:ehp2.
:dd.Access on-line help information
:edl.

:h3 res=10 id=The_IDE_Status_Bar.The IDE Status Bar
:i1.status bar, Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, status bar
.br
The Status Bar appears along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  This bar displays hint text for the 
toolbar button and menu option over which your cursor is positioned&per.  Status messages, such as Saving, Adding, and Executing 
Log, appear in this bar so you are aware of what action the IDE is performing&per.  A check mark beside the Show Status Bar 
menu option in the File menu indicates that this feature is enabled&per.  To disable this feature, choose the option again 
from the File menu and the status bar disappears from the window&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide2.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 2&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. The IDE Status Bar can be toggled from the File menu&per.:ehp2.

:h3 res=11 id=The_IDE_Toolbar.The IDE Toolbar
:i1.make all targets
:i1.profile target
:i1.sample target
:i1.browse target
:i1.remote debug target
:i1.debug target
:i1.run target
:i1.make target
:i1.make source
:i1.edit text
:i1 id=16.save project
:i2 refid=16.The IDE Toolbar
:i1 id=15.open project
:i2 refid=15.The IDE Toolbar
:i1 id=14.new project
:i2 refid=14.The IDE Toolbar
:i1.tool bar, Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, tool bar
.br
In the Open Watcom IDE Project window, the toolbar appears below the menu bar when the Show Tool Bar option in the File 
menu is activated (default)&per.  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled&per.  To disable this 
feature, choose the option again from the File menu and the toolbar disappears&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide3.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 3&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. You can access thirteen IDE functions from the toolbar&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The following explains the function each icon performs, as well as the equivalent function on the menu bar&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Create:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='newproj.bmp'. Create a new project&per.  This is equivalent to the option New Project in the File menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Open:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='openproj.bmp'. Open an existing project&per.  This is equivalent to the option Open Project in the File 
menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Save:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='saveproj.bmp'. Save the current project&per.  This is equivalent to the option Save Project in the File 
menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Edit:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='edititem.bmp'. Edit the selected source file&per.  This is equivalent to the option Edit Text in the 
Sources menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Make Source:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='makeitem.bmp'. Make the selected source file&per.  This is equivalent to the option Make in the Sources 
menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Make Target:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='maketarg.bmp'. Make the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Make in the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Run:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='runtarg.bmp'. Run the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Run in the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Debug:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='debgtarg.bmp'. Debug the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Debug in the Targets 
menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Remote Debug:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='rdbgtarg.bmp'. Debug the current target on a remote machine&per.  This is equivalent to the option Remote 
Debug in the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Browse:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='browtarg.bmp'. Browse the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Browse in the Targets 
menu&per.  Currently, browsing is supported by the Open Watcom C/C++ compilers only (Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 does not support 
browsing)&per.
:dt.:hp2.Sample:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='samptarg.bmp'. Run and sample the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Sample in the 
Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Profile:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='proftarg.bmp'. Profile the current target&per.  This is equivalent to the option Profile in the Targets 
menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.Make All:ehp2.
:dd.:artwork runin name='makeproj.bmp'. Make all targets in the project&per.  This is equivalent to the option Make All in the 
Actions menu&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=12 id=Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial.Getting Started with a Short Tutorial
:i1.tutorial, Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, tutorial
.br
Perhaps the easiest way to understand how the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment manages an entire project's 
files and tools, is to work through a brief example&per.  In this walk-through, you create and execute a simple C program 
using the IDE&per.  FORTRAN developers can follow along substituting FORTRAN code and file names where appropriate&per.  
The session will help you understand the basic concepts of the IDE and the steps involved in project development&per.
.br
.br
The illustrations shown in this section are taken from the Windows environment&per.  The OS/2 environment is very 
similar with some minor differences&per.  For example, in the OS/2 environment the IDE uses the OS/2 Editor rather than Open 
Watcom's Windows Editor&per.  Although this tutorial builds a Win32 executable, you can choose to build a Win16 or OS/2 PM 
executable&per.  The procedures to follow are not that much different&per.  When the time comes to select the type of target, 
simply choose an environment that you can run on your machine so that you can run your application to make sure it works&per.

:h3 res=13 id=A_Simple_Executable.A Simple Executable
:i2 refid=16.A Simple Executable
:i1 id=30.auto refresh
:i2 refid=30.A Simple Executable
:i2 refid=14.A Simple Executable
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, defining a project
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Double-click on the IDE icon:ehp2. in the Open Watcom Tools window&per.
.br
.br
The Open Watcom IDE Project window appears&per.  
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Project:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The Enter Project Filename dialog appears&per.  The default project name is:font facename=Courier size=12x10. noname&per.wpj&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the filename:ehp2.:font facename=Courier size=12x10. \project\first:font facename=Courier size=0x0. in the File 
Name field and click on OK&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide4.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 4&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Enter the filename in the Enter Project Filename dialog&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
This is the name of the project you are creating&per.  If you enter a directory that doesn't exist (in this case,:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
\project:font facename=Courier size=0x0.), the IDE reveals a message box asking if you want to create the specified directory&per. 
 Click on OK to create the specified directory and project file&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Note&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.The IDE looks for existing projects with the new name&per.  If a project with the specified name already exists, the IDE 
will prompt you rather than simply overwriting the existing project&per.
:edl.
.br
The project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  Note that the IDE added the extension 
"&per.wpj" to the project name&per.  The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you define a target for the new project&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the desired target name:ehp2. in the Target Name field (we suggest:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1:font facename=Courier size=0x0.)&per. 
 Next, select the appropriate Target Environment (we suggest "Win32") and Image Type (we suggest "Character-mode Executable")&per. 
 Click on OK when completed&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide5.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 5&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Select target name, target environment and image type:ehp2.
.br
.br
The New Target dialog closes and the IDE creates a target window entitled:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
for the new target&per.  Note that the IDE appends the appropriate extension to the target name for you once you click on 
OK&per.  You can now add sources to the target&per.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the sources you want to add to the target&per.  Source names must 
be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required&per.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename 
you enter&per.  You can also use the file browser to add files by clicking on the Browse&per.&per.&per.  button&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field or you 
can use the file browser to select files&per.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying 
the List Files of Type field&per.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field or selected a name from the file list, 
you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button&per.  All files in the file list can 
be added by clicking the Add All button&per.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking 
the Remove button&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide6.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 6&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Select source file names in the Add File(s) dialog:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(6):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the following source file names&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.      :font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     source1&per.c   (or source1&per.for):font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     source2&per.c   (or source2&per.for):font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     source3&per.rc:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space&per.  Click on OK when done&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after 
each one has been entered&per.  Click on Close when done&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide7.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 7&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Enter one or several source files in the Add File(s) dialog&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The IDE adds the new source files to the target window, followed by the characters [n/a]&per.  This indicates that 
the source files are not available, meaning that the files do not yet exist&per.  The indicators are&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.[n/a]:ehp2.
:dd.This code appears if the file does not exist&per.
:dt.:hp2.[r/o]:ehp2.
:dd.This code appears if the file is Read Only&per.
:dt.:hp2.[sw]:ehp2.
:dd.This code appears if the user has set switches for the specified source file&per.
:edl.
.br
The Integrated Development Environment organizes the source files in the target window under headers according to the 
filename extension&per.  You can expand and collapse the file groups by clicking on the folder icon&per.
.br
.br
For this tutorial, we only need one source file, so now we will delete the extra files from the target&per.
:dt.:hp2.(7):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the file:ehp2.:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source2&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. (or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
source2&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0.) to select it then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears asking if you want to remove the selected source&per.  Click on the Yes button to continue&per. 
 The IDE removes the selected source file from the target window&per.  Now we can remove:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
source3&per.rc&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  To do this we will perform the function of removing all source files 
with a common extension&per.
:dt.:hp2.(8):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the (&per.rc) header:ehp2. on the target window then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears on the screen asking if you want to remove all of the "&per.rc" files&per.  Click on the Yes 
button to continue&per.  The message box closes and the IDE removes the:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source3&per.rc:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
file from the target window&per.  Now you can edit the remaining source file&per.
:dt.:hp2.(9):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Highlight the line:ehp2.:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. (or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0.) and choose Edit Text from the Sources menu to edit the selected source file&per.
.br
.br
The Open Watcom Editor appears&per.  The name of the selected source file,:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
(or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0.), appears in the window&per.  Here 
you can enter the source code&per.
.br
.br
:hp3.Hint&colon.:ehp3.  You can also invoke the Open Watcom Editor by double-clicking on the source file that you 
want to edit&per.
.br
.br
In this case, the Open Watcom Editor appears because the selected source file has a "&per.c" or "&per.for" extension&per. 
 Performing an edit can open different editors, depending on the extension of the selected source file&per.
:dt.:hp2.(10):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the following lines of source code&per.:ehp2.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Important&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.For the purpose of this session, we ask you to enter:hp2. ii:ehp2. instead of:hp2. i:ehp2. in the "print" line&per.  This 
will allow you to see how the Integrated Development Environment reacts to errors&per.  You will then be able to correct 
the error&per.
:edl.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.      :font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     #include <stdio&per.h>:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     void main():font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     {:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         int i;:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         for (i&eq.0; i<10; i++):font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         {:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.             printf( "Value is %d\n", ii );:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         }:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         printf("Press Enter&per.&per.&per.\n");:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.         getchar();:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     }:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
FORTRAN programmers can enter the following lines of code&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.      :font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           program main:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           implicit none:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           integer i:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           do i &eq. 0, 9:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.             print *, 'Value is ', ii:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           enddo:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           print *, 'Press Enter&per.&per.&per.':font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           read *:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.           end:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
You can now save your work and close the Editor in one of the following two ways&colon.  close down the entire editor 
or close down only the source file window&per.  For this exercise, close only the source file window as follows&per.
:dt.:hp2.(11):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Close:ehp2. from the File menu to close down the current source file window&per.
.br
.br
In both cases, if changes have been made to the file and not saved, a message prompts you to save the changes before 
exiting&per.  The advantage of closing only the Editor window is that the Editor continues to run&per.  If you need to access 
it again, you will not need to wait for it to go through the start-up procedures again&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Important&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.You must save the file&per.  The Open Watcom compilers do not access the source lines in the Editor's buffers&per.
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.(12):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Minimize the Editor window&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the Open Watcom IDE Project window:ehp2. to bring it to the foreground&per.
:dt.:hp2.(13):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Refresh:ehp2. from the Window menu&per.
.br
.br
This action forces the IDE to update the display with the state of all files&per.  It is not necessary to use this 
function if you have selected Auto Refresh in the File menu&per.  If Auto Refresh is disabled then it may be necessary to 
alert the IDE of the presence of the file on disk&per.  
:dt.:hp2.(14):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Save Project:ehp2. from the File menu to save your project up to this point&per.
.br
.br
You are now ready to make and run the target&per.  Making a target is the act of constructing a target file&per. 
 In this case, it involves compiling the source file:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
(or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0.) using the appropriate Open Watcom 
compiler and linking the target executable using the Open Watcom Linker&per.
:dt.:hp2.(15):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Highlight the line:ehp2.:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. and choose 
C Compiler Switches&per.&per.&per.  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options&per.
.br
.br
:hp2.FORTRAN programmers&colon.:ehp2.  Highlight the line:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
and choose Fortran Compiler Switches&per.&per.&per.  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile 
options&per.
.br
.br
The C Compiler Switches dialog appears&per.  If you are using FORTRAN, the Fortran Compiler Switches dialog appears&per.
:dt.:hp2.(16):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the ">>" button:ehp2. until the:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 7&per.  CodeGen Strategy Switches:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
dialog appears&per.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox button to select this option&per.
.br
.br
:hp2.FORTRAN programmers&colon.:ehp2.  Click on the ">>" button until the:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 9&per. 
 Application Type Switches:font facename=Courier size=0x0. dialog appears&per.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" 
checkbox to select this option&per.
.br
.br
A check-mark appears in the checkbox&per.
.br
.br
Note&colon.  Selecting this option is for demonstration purposes only, it is not required for this simple application&per.
.br
.br
Click OK to close the dialog&per.
:dt.:hp2.(17):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE constructs a makefile based on its knowledge of the target and construction rules and then invokes the Open 
Watcom Make utility to create:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  The 
IDE Log window appears at the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  Use this window to monitor the procedure&per. 
 Since you entered:hp2. ii:ehp2. instead of:hp2. i,:ehp2. the MAKE fails&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide8.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 8&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(18):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Highlight the error message:ehp2. in the IDE Log window and choose Help on Message from the Log menu&per.
.br
Help information regarding the error message appears on the screen&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide9.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 9&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Help for error messages is available:ehp2.
.br
.br
Close the Help window and edit the source file as follows&per.
:dt.:hp2.(19):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Highlight the error message:ehp2. in the IDE Log window and choose Edit File from the Log menu&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Double-click on the error message:ehp2. in the IDE Log window&per.
.br
.br
The Open Watcom Editor opens and the cursor appears at the position of the error&per.  Edit and correct the error 
and then save the file again&per.  Minimize the Editor window and bring the IDE window to the foreground&per.
:dt.:hp2.(20):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Targets menu again&per.
.br
.br
This time, the procedure creates the executable file:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
:dt.:hp2.(21):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Run:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE executes the program:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide10.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 10&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Run from the Targets menu runs the target&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The program you wrote is a character mode application and its output appears in a separate console window&per.  You 
are now ready to debug your target program&per.  You probably do not need to debug this simple program with the Open Watcom 
Debugger but go through the steps so that you know what to do for more complex programs&per.
.br
.br
The IDE has several debugging information options which it uses as defaults when it is started&per.  The first thing 
to do is check to make sure that your application compiled with the default debugging information activated&per.
:dt.:hp2.(22):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu to activate the Target Options menu&per.
.br
.br
The option Use Development Switches should appear in grey text with a check mark next to it&per.  Development Switches 
automatically include debugging information in the target&per.  If you were to select Use Release Switches, the IDE would 
select options useful for a production version of your program&per.  Selecting this option turns the text grey and places 
a check mark beside it&per.  The IDE uses the development switches by default&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Note&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.For the purpose of this session, you should make the target with the development switches on&per.  If this is not the option 
selected, select it and remake the target before debugging&per.
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.(23):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Debug:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE starts the Open Watcom Debugger&per.
:dt.:hp2.(24):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Step Over:ehp2. from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to step through the code&per.  Choose:hp2. Go:ehp2. 
from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to continue execution to the end of the code&per.  For more information on 
using the Open Watcom Debugger, refer to the:hp3. Open Watcom Debugger User's Guide&per.:ehp3.:hp2.  The task has completed 
running:ehp2. appears when the program has completed execution&per.
:dt.:hp2.(25):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Exit:ehp2. from the File menu to terminate the debugging session&per.
.br
.br
The Open Watcom Debugger closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  You can now exit the IDE&per.
:dt.:hp2.(26):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Exit:ehp2. from the File menu to exit the IDE&per.
.br
.br
The IDE prompts you to save the current project if you have not saved it since the last change&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=14 id=Review_of_What_Happened.Review of What Happened
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, tutorial review
.br
In this session, you created a project called:font facename=Courier size=12x10. first&per.wpj&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
 This project had one target called:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe,:font facename=Courier size=0x0. a 
32-bit Windows executable (unless you specified another target type)&per.  Using the Open Watcom Editor, you entered and 
edited the source text in a file called:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0. (if you tried the FORTRAN example)&per.  You compiled and linked it into 
an executable program using the Open Watcom Make utility, a Open Watcom compiler, and the Open Watcom Linker&per.  You ran 
it directly and under the control of the Open Watcom Debugger&per.
.br
.br
When you saved the project, the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment created the following permanent files&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.first&per.wpj:ehp2.
:dd.Describes the screen layout and refers to a file called:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.tgt:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
:dt.:hp2.target1&per.tgt:ehp2.
:dd.Describes the target executable:font facename=Courier size=12x10. target1&per.exe:font facename=Courier size=0x0. and all 
switches required to link it&per.  It also describes:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
(or:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source1&per.for:font facename=Courier size=0x0.) and the switches required to compile 
it&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=15 id=The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window.The Open Watcom IDE Project Window
:i1.project window, Integrated Development Environment
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, project window
.br
When you first start the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, an empty Open Watcom IDE Project window opens&per. 
 This window represents the project that is currently open in the IDE&per.  The name of the current project appears in the 
title bar of this window&per.
.br
.br
The first thing you need to do is open a project for the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  This can be a new project 
you create or an existing project you open&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Important&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.You can have only one project open at a time in the IDE&per.  If you attempt to open or create a project while one already 
exists in the IDE window, a message box appears informing you that the IDE is closing the current project and asks if you 
want to save any changes made to that project&per.  Choose Yes, No, or Cancel in this box to continue&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=16 id=Opening_a_Project.Opening a Project
:i2 refid=15.Opening a Project
:i2 refid=14.Opening a Project
:i1.opening a project
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, opening a project
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To create a new project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Project:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The Enter Project Filename dialog appears on the screen where you enter the name of the project you are creating&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the desired name:ehp2. in the File Name field and choose the desired directory&per.
.br
.br
All projects must have the extension "&per.wpj"&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide11.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 11&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the Enter Project Filename dialog to create a new project&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when completed&per.
.br
.br
The Enter Project Filename dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE 
Project window&per.  A New Target dialog opens on the screen where you add a target to the new project&per.  Refer to the 
section Adding a Target for more information&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To open an existing project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Open Project:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The Open dialog appears on the screen where you select the project you want to open&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide12.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 12&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the Open dialog to open an existing project&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose the desired project&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
All projects have the extension "&per.wpj"&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when the desired project appears in the File Name field&per.
.br
.br
The Open dialog closes and the name of the selected project appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project 
window&per.  If targets exist for the selected project, windows representing the targets appear in the main window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=17 id=Closing_a_Project.Closing a Project
:i1.close project
:i1.closing a project
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, closing a project
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To close the current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Close Project:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE removes all target windows for the current project from the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  The project 
name disappears from the title bar and the window is empty&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=18 id=Saving_a_Project.Saving a Project
:i1.saving a project
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, saving a project
.br
There are two ways of saving your current project&per.  You can perform a simple save where the project is updated with 
any changes made since the last save or you can save the project under a different project name using the Save Project As 
function&per.

:h4 res=19 id=Updating_the_Project.Updating the Project
:i2 refid=16.Updating the Project
:i1.updating a project
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, updating a project
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To perform a simple save on your project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Save Project:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE updates the project specified in the title bar&per.  The status bar along the bottom of the window indicates 
that the save function is being executed&per.
:edl.

:h4 res=20 id=Saving_the_Project_Under_a_New_Name.Saving the Project Under a New Name
:i1.save project as
:i1.saving a project under a new name
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, saving a project under a new name
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To save the current project using a different name&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Save Project As:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The Save As dialog appears on the screen where you specify the file name by which you want to save the current project&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the desired project name:ehp2. in the File Name field&per.
.br
.br
This is the new name by which you want to save the current project&per.  This can be the name of a new project or 
an existing project&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Important&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.Entering the name of an existing project overwrites the contents of that project with the contents of the current project&per.
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose the desired directory:ehp2. for the project using the Directories list on the Save As dialog&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when completed&per.
.br
.br
The Save As dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=21 id=Working_with_Targets.Working with Targets
:i1 id=47.targets
:i2 refid=47.Working with Targets
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, targets
.br
Once you have created a project, it is a simple matter to add additional targets&per.  You can also change the name of 
a target, remove targets, change the target environment, and change the image type&per.  This section discusses the following 
topics&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Adding a Target
:li.Minimizing a Target
:li.Removing a Target
:li.Renaming a Target
:li.Marking a Target for Remake
:li.Making Targets
:li.Running a Target
:eul.

:h3 res=22 id=Adding_a_Target.Adding a Target
:i1.targets, adding
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, adding a target
.br
You add targets to the current project using the New Target dialog&per.  When you create a new project, this dialog appears 
automatically&per.  You have two options when adding targets to a project&colon.  you can either enter the name of a new 
target or you can choose an existing target, previously created in another project&per.  This section describes both methods 
for adding a target&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add a new target to the current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Target:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you enter the details of the target you are adding&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide13.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 13&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Enter the new target details in the New Target dialog&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the target name:ehp2. in the Target Name field&per.
.br
.br
Enter the entire pathname to ensure the directory is created where you want&per.  Depending on the nature of the 
target, it may or may not be able to share the same directory as other pre-existing targets&per.  Generally, if the target 
shares one or more of the same source files as other targets in the project, then it must be placed in its own subdirectory&per. 
 The object files that are produced for the target may have different attributes than a pre-existing target's object files 
(e&per.g&per., 16-bit vs 32-bit)&per.  This is why the target should be placed in a separate subdirectory&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select the desired Target Environment and Image Type:ehp2. as desired&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when the Target Name, Target Environment and Image Type fields are correct&per.
.br
.br
If you specify a new directory in the target pathname, a message box appears asking if you want to create the specified 
directory&per.  Choose Yes or No to continue&per.  A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project 
window&per.  The target name appears in the title bar&per.
.br
.br
The first line in this window describes the target&per.  It specifies the current mode, the target type&per.  The 
current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type&per.  The 
letter D indicates that the target is placed in Development mode&per.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in 
Release mode&per.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target&per.
:edl.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide14.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 14&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. A project may have multiple targets&per.:ehp2.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add an existing target to a current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Target:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The New Target dialog appears on the screen&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the Browse button&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The Enter Target Filename dialog appears on the screen&per.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and 
files and select the "&per.tgt" file for the target you want to add&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide15.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 15&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Choose an existing target from the Pick Target Filename dialog for the current 
project&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the desired target:ehp2. to select it&per.
.br
.br
The selected target appears in the File Name field&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to process the target selection&per.
.br
.br
The Enter Target Filename dialog closes&per.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to close the New Target dialog&per.
.br
.br
A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  The target name appears 
in the title bar&per.
.br
.br
The first line in this window describes the target&per.  It specifies the current mode and the target type&per.  
The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type&per. 
 The letter D indicates that the target is placed in development mode&per.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed 
in Release mode&per.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=23 id=Minimizing_a_Target.Minimizing a Target
:i1.minimizing a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, minimizing a target
.br
If you are working with a project that has multiple targets, the Open Watcom IDE Project window can become cluttered&per. 
 You can tidy your window by minimizing the target windows that you are not currently using&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To minimize a target window&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the system box:ehp2. in the upper left-hand corner of the target window you want to close&per.
.br
.br
A menu appears listing the functions you can perform on the selected window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Minimize:ehp2. from the menu&per.
.br
.br
The selected target window closes and an icon appears along the bottom of the screen representing that window&per. 
 The name of the target appears on or under the icon&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Double-click on the icon:ehp2. to open the target window again&per.
:edl.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide16.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 16&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Minimize targets to reduce clutter in the project window&per.:ehp2.

:h3 res=24 id=Removing_a_Target.Removing a Target
:i1.removing a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, removing a target
.br
The procedure for removing a target from a project is simple&per.  If your project has multiple targets, it is important 
to ensure that the target you want to remove is the one selected&per.  Removing a target does not permanently delete the 
target, it simply removes it from the current project&per.  If the target is attached to multiple projects, those projects 
will not be affected by the removal of the selected target from the current project&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To remove a target from the current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. you want to remove from the project&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Remove Target:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears on the screen asking if you really want to remove the selected target&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose OK:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
The message box closes and the selected target window disappears from the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=25 id=Renaming_a_Target.Renaming a Target
:i1.renaming a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, renaming a target
.br
When renaming a target, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing target&per.  Choosing 
an existing target name overwrites the contents of the existing target with the contents of the current target&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To rename a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. you want to rename&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Rename Target:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The Rename Target dialog appears&per.  The Target Name, Target Environment, and Image Type of the selected target 
appear in the appropriate fields&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide17.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 17&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Change a target's name using the Rename Target dialog&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the new target name:ehp2. in the Target Name field&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the Browse button:ehp2. and select an existing target name; click on OK when completed&per.
.br
.br
The selected or entered target name appears in the Target Name field&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Verify that the Target Environment and Image Type are correct&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. on the Rename Target window&per.
.br
.br
The Rename Target dialog closes&per.  The title bar of the selected target window now displays the new target name&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=26 id=Marking_a_Target_for_Remake.Marking a Target for Remake
:i1.marking a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, marking a target
.br
Marking a target for remake ensures that the IDE will perform the link the next time you make that target or make all 
targets&per.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Setup Target submenu under the 
Targets menu&per.  By marking a target for remake, you control when and which targets need to be remade&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To mark a target for remake&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. you want to mark&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Mark Target for Remake:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the selected target&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Yes or No:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
Choosing Yes marks all of the source files for the selected target as well as the target itself&per.  This means 
that the next time you do a make, the source files for the selected target will be compiled&per.  Choosing No marks only 
the selected target for remake&per.  In both cases, the message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project 
window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=27 id=Making_Targets.Making Targets
:i1.making a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, making a target
.br
The Make function in the Targets menu creates an executable for the selected target&per.  When you perform this function, 
the IDE compiles the source files for the selected target into object files&per.  The object files are then linked together 
to create the target file&per.
.br
.br
There are two make functions available to you for targets&colon.  Make and Make All&per.  The Make All function in 
the Actions menu tells the IDE to compile and link all of the targets for the current project&per.  This can be a lengthy 
process, depending upon the number of targets in your project, so you may choose to only make targets to which changes have 
been made&per.  This enables you to see if they can be made successfully&per.
.br
.br
Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To make a single target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. you want to make to select it&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  In the log window you can monitor 
the make procedure&per.:hp2.  Execution Complete:ehp2. appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected 
target&per.  If the make is successful an executable file for the selected target can be found in the directory associated 
with the target window&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide18.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 18&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure&per.:ehp2.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To make all targets for the current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make All:ehp2. from the Actions Menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  In the log window you can monitor 
the make procedure&per.:hp2.  Execution Complete:ehp2. appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets&per. 
 If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with 
the target window&per.
:edl.

:h4 res=28 id=Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target.Viewing the Make Command for a Target
:i1 id=61.viewing make commands
:i2 refid=61.Viewing the Make Command for a Target
:i1 id=60.Integrated Development Environment, viewing make commands
:i2 refid=60.Viewing the Make Command for a Target
.br
With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected target&per. 
 These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected target when you choose the function Make from the Targets 
menu or Make All from the Actions menu&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To view the make commands for targets&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. whose makefile commands you want to view&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu appears on the screen from the Target Options menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Show Make Command:ehp2. from the Target Options submenu&per.
.br
.br
The Target Command dialog appears on the screen&per.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected 
target&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide19.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 19&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. The Target Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to close the dialog&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=29 id=Running_a_Target.Running a Target
:i1.running a target
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, running a target
.br
Once you have compiled and linked your target using the Make function, you can run the target to execute the program&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To run a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. you want to run&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Run:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE executes the target program&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide20.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 20&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Run from the Targets menu runs the target&per.:ehp2.
:edl.

:h4 res=30 id=Other_Run_Functions.Other Run Functions
:i1.running programs
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, running programs
.br
In the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment you can run programs other than the targets in your current project 
window&per.  Use the run functions in the Actions menu to specify the program you want to run&per.  The Run item runs the 
specified program under your host environment&per.  The Run Batch item runs the specified program in the IDE Log window&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To run other programs in the IDE&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Run:ehp2. from the Actions menu&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Run Batch:ehp2. from the Actions menu&per.
.br
.br
A Run or Run Batch dialog appears on the screen&per.  These dialogs are identical&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide21.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 21&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the Run (or Run Batch) dialog to run other programs in the IDE&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the name of the program:ehp2. you want to run in the Command field&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to run the specified program&per.
.br
.br
The dialog closes and the specified program runs, either under the host environment or in the IDE Log window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=31 id=Setting_Application_Parameters.Setting Application Parameters
:i1.setting options
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, setting options
.br
The Targets and Sources menus and their submenus are divided into sections&per.  Horizontal lines separate each section&per. 
 The menu items in the bottom sections of the Targets and Sources menus are actions that you can perform on the selected 
target or source file in the current project&per.  If there are switches that can be set for these actions, there are corresponding 
menu item entries in the bottom sections of the submenus&per.  Choose Target Options and Source Options from their respective 
menus to display the submenus&per.

:h2 res=32 id=Working_with_Source_Files.Working with Source Files
:i1.source files
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, source files
.br
Once your project has a target you need to add source files to the target&per.  The procedure for adding a source file 
to a target is simple&per.  Once you have source files that make up your target, you can also change the name of a source 
file and remove source files&per.  This section discusses the following topics&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Adding Source Files
:li.Removing Source Files
:li.Renaming a Source File
:li.Marking a Source File for Remake
:li.Making Source Files
:li.Viewing Included Files
:eul.

:h3 res=33 id=Adding_Source_Files.Adding Source Files
:i1.extensions, &per.sqp
:i1.extensions, &per.sqc
:i1.extensions, &per.res
:i1.extensions, &per.rc
:i1.extensions, &per.hlp
:i1.extensions, &per.for
:i1.extensions, &per.cpp
:i1.extensions, &per.c
:i1.extensions, &per.asm
:i1.source files, adding
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, adding source files
.br
You add source files to the selected target (the target whose title bar is currently highlighted) using the Add File(s) 
dialog&per.  The current project must have a target in order to use the New Source function&per.  You have two options when 
adding source files to a target&colon.  you can either enter the name of a new source file or you can choose an existing 
source file&per.  When adding existing source files you can use the file browser, type sources one at a time into the Add 
File(s) dialog, or use wild cards in the Add File(s) dialog&per.  This section describes all methods for adding source files&per.
.br
.br
The extension you use with the source file name determines the compiler that the IDE uses to create the target&per. 
 For example, the "&per.c" extension, invokes the Open Watcom C compiler to make the target&per.  You can specify several 
different compilers&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.asm:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.asm" extension invokes the Open Watcom Assembler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.c:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.c" extension invokes the Open Watcom C compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.cpp:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.cpp" extension invokes the Open Watcom C++ compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.for:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.for" extension invokes the Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.hlp:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.hlp" extension invokes either the Win16, OS/2, or Win32 compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.rc:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.rc" extension invokes the Open Watcom resource compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.res:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.res" extension invokes the Open Watcom Resource Editor&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.sqc:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.sqc" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C compiler&per.
:dt.:hp2.xxx&per.sqp:ehp2.
:dd.The "&per.sqp" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C++ compiler&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add new source files to the selected target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the name(s) of the source file(s) you are adding&per.  Source names 
must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required&per.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename 
you enter into the filenames field&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field, one 
at a time&per.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field, you can add it to the list of source files to be included 
by clicking the Add button&per.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove 
button&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide22.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 22&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Add one or several source files to a target using the Add File(s) dialog&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the source file name(s)&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space&per.  Click on OK when done&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after 
each one has been entered&per.  Click on Close when done&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom 
IDE Project window&per.
.br
.br
The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order&per.  You can expand and collapse 
the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add existing source files to the selected target using the file browser&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen&per.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, click on the Browse button and the Pick Filename dialog appears on the screen&per.  This dialog 
allows you to browse the directories and files and select the source file(s) you want to add to the current target&per.  
Click on the desired source file to select it&per.  The selected source file(s) appear highlighted in the Pick Filename dialog&per. 
 When done browsing files, click on OPEN to add the selected files&per.  The Pick Filename dialog closes&per.  When done 
selecting files, click on OK&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, you can use the file browser to select files&per.  The type of files displayed in file list 
window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field&per.  Once you have selected a name from the file list, 
you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button&per.  All files in the file list can 
be added by clicking the Add All button&per.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking 
the Remove button&per.  When done, click on Close to add the selected files&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom 
IDE Project window&per.
.br
.br
The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order&per.  You can expand and collapse 
the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add existing source files to the selected target using wild cards&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose New Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen&per.
.br
.br
For the OS/2 IDE, enter the source file name pattern in the filename(s) field using wild cards&per.  For example, 
enter:font facename=Courier size=12x10. sou*&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. to add all source files whose name begins 
"sou" and have a "&per.c" extension to the current target&per.  When done, click on OK to add the selected files&per.
.br
.br
:hp3.Hint&colon.:ehp3.  Even when entering wild cards, you can add multiple source file groups by separating each 
entry with one space (e&per.g&per., sou*&per.c sou*&per.cpp)&per.
.br
.br
For all other systems, enter the source file name pattern in the File Name field using wild cards&per.  For example, 
enter:font facename=Courier size=12x10. sou*&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. to select all source files whose name 
begins "sou" and have a "&per.c" extension&per.  Click on the Add All button to add all the names that match the wild card 
pattern&per.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button&per.  
When done, click on Close to add the selected files&per.
.br
.br
The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom 
IDE Project window&per.
.br
.br
The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order&per.  You can expand and collapse 
the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=34 id=Removing_a_Source_File.Removing a Source File
:i1.source files, removing
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, removing source files
.br
The procedure for removing a source file from a project is simple&per.  You can remove one source at a time or you can 
remove all source files with a common file extension from the target&per.  Removing a source file does not permanently delete 
the it&per.  The IDE simply removes it from the current target&per.  If the removed source file is common to multiple targets, 
the other targets will not be affected by the removal of the selected source file&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To remove one source file from a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. you want to remove from the target&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Remove Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears asking if you are sure you want to remove the selected source file&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the Yes button&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected source file from the target window&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To remove a group of source files from a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the header:ehp2. to the right of the folder icon of the source files group you want to remove from the 
target&per.
.br
.br
The selected header appears highlighted in the target window&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide23.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 23&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Click on the header to remove all source files with a common file extension&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Remove Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A message window appears asking if you want to remove all source files with the selected file extension&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose OK:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected group of source files from the target&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=35 id=Renaming_a_Source_File.Renaming a Source File
:i1.source files, renaming
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, renaming source files
.br
When renaming a source file, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing source file&per. 
 Choosing an existing source file name overwrites the contents of that source file with the contents of the current source 
file&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To rename a source file&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. you want to rename&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Rename Source:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Rename File dialog appears where you enter the new name of the selected source file&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide24.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 24&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the Rename File dialog to rename the selected source file&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the new source file name:ehp2. in the field&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the Browse button:ehp2. and select an existing source file name; click on OK when completed&per.
.br
.br
The selected or entered source file name appears in the Rename File dialog&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. on the Rename File dialog&per.
.br
.br
The Rename File dialog closes&per.  The target window displays the updated source file&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=36 id=Marking_a_Source_File_for_Remake.Marking a Source File for Remake
:i1.source files, marking for remake
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, marking source files for remake
.br
The Mark Source for Remake function in the Sources menu enables you to mark a source file to ensure that the IDE will 
recompile it the next time you make the target&per.  This function is useful because although changes may not have been made 
to the selected source file, changes may have been made to a source file on which the selected source file depends&per.  
This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per. 
 Marking source files for remake gives you control over when and which source files need to be remade&per.
.br
.br
With the Mark function you can mark an individual source file or a group of source files with a common file extension&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To mark a source file or a group of source files for remake&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. you want to mark&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the header:ehp2. of the group of source files you want to mark&per.
.br
.br
The selection is highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Mark Source for Remake:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
If you selected a group of source files to mark, a message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the 
source files of the specified extension&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Yes:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
The message box disappears and the IDE marks all of the source files in the selected group to be recompiled&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=37 id=Making_Source_Files.Making Source Files
:i1.source files, making
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, making source files
.br
The Make function in the Sources menu enables you to compile a selected source file into an object file&per.  You can 
also perform the Make function on a group of source files with a common file extension&per.
.br
.br
Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To make source files&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. you want to make&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the header:ehp2. of the group of source files you want to make&per.
.br
.br
The selected source files are highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  In the log window you can monitor 
the make procedure&per.:hp2.  Execution Complete:ehp2. appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected 
source files&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To make all targets for the current project&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make All:ehp2. from the Actions Menu&per.
.br
.br
The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  In the log window you can monitor 
the make procedure&per.:hp2.  Execution Complete:ehp2. appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets&per. 
 If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with 
the target window&per.
:edl.

:h4 res=38 id=Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Source_File.Viewing the Make Command for a Source File
:i2 refid=61.Viewing the Make Command for a Source File
:i2 refid=60.Viewing the Make Command for a Source File
.br
With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected source file&per. 
 These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected source file when you choose the function Make from the 
Sources menu&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To view the make commands for source files&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. whose makefile commands you want to view&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the header:ehp2. of the group of source files whose makefile commands you want to view&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file or group header is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Source Options:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu appears on the screen from the Source Options menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Show Make Command:ehp2. from the Source Options submenu&per.
.br
.br
The Source Command dialog appears on the screen&per.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected 
source file or source file header&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide25.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 25&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. The IDE Source Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected 
source file(s)&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to close the dialog&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=39 id=Viewing_Included_Files.Viewing Included Files
:i1.viewing included files list
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, viewing included files list
.br
The Included Files function in the Sources menu enables you to select a source file and view a list of all of the files 
included by that source file&per.  This is a list of all files on which the selected source file depends&per.  From this 
list you can select included files and edit them&per.
.br
.br
Because the selected source file depends on the files in this list, it is necessary to remake it if changes are made 
to any of the included files&per.  If you are using the Autodepend function, the IDE automatically remakes the source file 
if any of the included files are modified&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To view the list of included files&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the source file:ehp2. whose list of included files you want to view&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file is highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Included Files:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
The Files Included dialog appears on the screen&per.  This dialog lists all source files on which the selected source 
files depends&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To edit a source file from the File Included dialog&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. in the included files list that you want to edit&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file is highlighted in the File Included dialog&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Double-click:ehp2. on the selected source file&per.
.br
.br
The Files Included dialog closes and the IDE invokes the appropriate editor for the selected included file&per.  
The editor appears and you can edit the file&per.  Close the editor when you are finished making the desired changes to return 
to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=40 id=Using_the_IDE_Log_Window.Using the IDE Log Window
:i1.log window, using the
:i1.using the log window
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, using the log window
.br
When you compile a target or source file, the IDE Log window appears along the bottom of the screen&per.  Once this window 
is open it remains on your screen until you close the project&per.  However, to maximize your project window space you can 
minimize the IDE Log window when it is not being used&per.
.br
.br
The Log menu contains three functions that you use in relation to the IDE Log window&per.  This section discusses 
the following functions&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Stopping the Make Function
:li.Getting Help
:li.Editing the Source File
:li.Saving the Log
:eul.

:h3 res=41 id=Stopping_the_Make_Function.Stopping the Make Function
:i1.stopping the make
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, stopping the make
.br
Choosing to make a source file, target, or all targets can be a lengthy process&per.  The Stop function in the Log menu 
gives you the opportunity to cancel the make function once it has started&per.  This is a useful function in case you decide 
that you do not want to continue with the make&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To stop the make function&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Stop:ehp2. from the Log menu&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears asking if it is okay to interrupt the executing process&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
The message box closes and the IDE stops the make function&per.  The IDE Log window displays the message:hp2. Stop 
Invoked:ehp2. followed by the message:hp2. Execution Complete&per.:ehp2.
:edl.

:h3 res=42 id=Getting_Help.Getting Help
:i1.getting help
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, getting help
.br
The Help on Message function in the Log menu gives you access to on-line help information for the selected error message 
in the IDE Log window&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To reveal help for an error message&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the error message:ehp2. in the IDE Log window on which you want information&per.
.br
.br
The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Help on Message:ehp2. from the Log menu&per.
.br
.br
This opens the Help window&per.  Information on the selected error message appears in this window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Close:ehp2. from the system menu on the Help window to cancel the Help facility&per.
.br
.br
The Help window closes and you return to the IDE Log window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=43 id=Editing_the_Source_File.Editing the Source File
:i1.source files, editing
:i1.editing source
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, editing source
.br
The Edit File function in the Log menu allows you to select an error message in the IDE Log window and edit the corresponding 
source file&per.  This function invokes the appropriate editor for the source file and positions your cursor in the source 
code where the error occurred (if the error message contained line and column information)&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To edit a source file&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the error message:ehp2. in the IDE Log window that you want to edit&per.
.br
.br
The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Edit File:ehp2. from the Log menu&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Double-click:ehp2. on the selected error message&per.
.br
.br
Both of these actions open the appropriate editor&per.  If the Open Watcom Editor appears and the error message contained 
column and line details, your cursor will be positioned at the error in the source code&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Make the required changes:ehp2. to the source file and exit the Editor&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=44 id=Saving_the_Log.Saving the Log
:i1.saving the log
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, saving the log
.br
The Save Log As function in the Log menu allows you to save the contents of the Log window into a file&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To save the log&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Save Log As:ehp2. from the Log menu&per.
.br
.br
The Save Log As dialog appears where you specify the filename by which you want to save the contents of the Log window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the desired log name:ehp2. in the File Name field&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Specify the directory:ehp2. to which you want to save the log&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on the OK button&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The IDE closes the Save Log As dialog and saves the current log as specified
:edl.

:h2 res=45 id=The_IDE_Switches.The IDE Switches
:i1 id=105.switches, setting
:i2 refid=105.The IDE Switches
:i1.setting switches
:i1 id=103.Integrated Development Environment, setting switches
:i2 refid=103.The IDE Switches
.br
There are a number of different switches you can set in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment&per.  Switches 
enables you to change the way the various tools, such as compilers and linkers, perform their jobs&per.  The switches discussed 
in this section are&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Release and Development Switches
:li.Target and Source File Switches
:li.The Autodepend Switch
:eul.
.br
This section discusses the different switches available in the IDE and how you can use them&per.

:h3 res=46 id=Using_Release_and_Development_Switches.Using Release and Development Switches
:i1.switches, development
:i1.development switches
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, development switches
:i1.switches, release
:i1.release switches
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, release switches
.br
There are two sets of switches the IDE can use during the make process&colon.  Development and Release switches&per. 
 You are able to specify different switches in each set for each target in your project&per.  The letters D and R in the 
target window to the left of the target type indicate which set of switches is being used&per.  You choose the switches you 
want the IDE to use from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.  The selected set of switches has a check 
mark beside it and appears in grey text&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To change the selected set of switches for a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target:ehp2. whose switches you want to change&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu appears from the Target Options menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Use Development Switches or Use Release Switches:ehp2. (whichever is not currently selected) from the submenu, 
as desired&per.
.br
.br
A message box appears asking if you want to mark the selected target and its source files for remake&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Yes or No,:ehp2. as desired, to continue&per.
.br
.br
The message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE window&per.  The next time you perform a make, the IDE 
will use the selected set of switches&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=47 id=Setting_Target_and_Source_File_Switches.Setting Target and Source File Switches
:i2 refid=105.Setting Target and Source File Switches
:i2 refid=103.Setting Target and Source File Switches
.br
You can set a variety of command-line compiler and linker options in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment&per. 
 This section shows you how to use the Switches dialogs to choose your options&per.
.br
.br
You can set the Integrated Development Environment to work in one of two switch modes&colon.  Use Development Switches 
or Use Release Switches&per.  Either of these two options uses a default set of switch settings, unless you specify otherwise&per. 
 You can set switches for an individual target, specific source file type, or individual source file&per.

:h4 res=48 id=Setting_Target_Switches.Setting Target Switches
:i1.switches, setting target
:i1.targets, setting switches
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, setting target switches
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To set switches for a target&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. for which you want to set switches&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected window is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Windows Linking Switches:ehp2. from the Options menu&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu and then choose Windows Linking Switches from the Target Options 
submenu&per.
.br
.br
The Windows Linking Switches dialog appears on the screen&per.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only 
the currently selected target&per.  There are four Switches dialogs, each one allowing you to set the following types of 
switches&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.Basic
:li.Import, Export and Library
:li.Advanced
:li.Special
:li.Resource
:eul.
.br
You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to 
set&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide26.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 26&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the Windows Linking Switches dialog to set command-line compiler and linker 
options&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The first dialog allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see&per.  Remember, if the option Use 
Development Switches is enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment 
automatically sets switches to include all debugging information&per.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release 
Switches from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyRel:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog&per. 
 You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected 
in the Setup Target submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyDev:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current 
dialog&per.  You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Release switches are 
selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the 
Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when you are finished setting switches for the selected target&per.
.br
.br
The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.

:h4 res=49 id=Setting_Source_File_Switches.Setting Source File Switches
:i1.switches, setting source file
:i1.source files, setting switches
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, setting source file switches
.br
The Switches function in the Source Options submenu of the Sources menu enables you to set switches for the compiler 
that is associated with the selected source file or source file group&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To set switches for a source file group&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target window:ehp2. that contains the relevant source files&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose the appropriate item:ehp2. from the Options menu&per.
.br
.br
For example, to set switches for all "&per.c" files in the selected target, choose C Compiler Switches from the Options 
menu&per.
.br
.br
The Compiler Switches dialog appears on the screen&per.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only the 
currently selected source file or source file group&per.  There are eleven Switches dialogs for Open Watcom C/C++, each one 
allowing you to set the following types of switches&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.File Option Switches
:li.Diagnostics Switches
:li.Source Switches
:li.Miscellaneous Switches
:li.Optimization Switches
:li.Debugging Switches
:li.CodeGen Strategy Switches
:li.Register Usage Switches
:li.CodeGen Option Switches
:li.Memory Model and Processor Switches
:li.C or C++ Compiler Switches
:eul.
.br
There are ten Switches dialogs for Open Watcom FORTRAN 77&per.
:ul compact.
:li.File Option Switches
:li.Compile-time Diagnostic Switches
:li.Run-time Diagnostic Switches
:li.Debugging Switches
:li.Code Generation Strategy Switches
:li.Code Generation Option Switches
:li.Register Usage Switches
:li.Optimization Switches
:li.Application Type Switches
:li.Miscellaneous Switches
:eul.
.br
You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to 
set&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide27.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 27&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the C Compiler Switches dialog to set switches for the compiler associated 
with the selected source file(s)&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The first of several compiler switch dialogs appears&per.  Of note is the sixth screen, Debugging Switches, which 
allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see&per.  Remember, if the option Use Development Switches is 
enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment automatically sets switches 
to include all debugging information&per.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release Switches from the Target 
Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyRel:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog&per. 
 You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected 
in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyDev:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current 
dialog&per.  You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Release switches are 
selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the 
Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when you are finished setting switches for the selected source file or group of source files&per.
.br
.br
The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To set switches for a specific source file&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the source file:ehp2. for which you want to set switches&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Source Options:ehp2. from the Sources menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu for the Source Options menu appears on the screen&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose the appropriate item:ehp2. from the Source Options submenu&per.
.br
.br
For example, if the selected source file has a "&per.c" extension, choose C Compiler Switches from the Options menu&per.
.br
.br
The Compiler Switches dialog appears on the screen&per.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only the 
currently selected source file&per.  There are eleven Switches dialogs for Open Watcom C/C++, each one allowing you to set 
the following types of switches&colon.
:ul compact.
:li.File Option Switches
:li.Diagnostics Switches
:li.Source Switches
:li.Miscellaneous Switches
:li.Optimization Switches
:li.Debugging Switches
:li.CodeGen Strategy Switches
:li.Register Usage Switches
:li.CodeGen Option Switches
:li.Memory Model and Processor Switches
:li.C or C++ Compiler Switches
:eul.
.br
There are ten Switches dialogs for Open Watcom FORTRAN 77&per.
:ul compact.
:li.File Option Switches
:li.Compile-time Diagnostic Switches
:li.Run-time Diagnostic Switches
:li.Debugging Switches
:li.Code Generation Strategy Switches
:li.Code Generation Option Switches
:li.Register Usage Switches
:li.Optimization Switches
:li.Application Type Switches
:li.Miscellaneous Switches
:eul.
.br
You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to 
set&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide28.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 28&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Use the C Compiler Switches dialog to set switches for the compiler associated 
with the selected source file(s)&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The first of several compiler switch dialogs appears&per.  Of note is the sixth screen, Debugging Switches, which 
allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see&per.  Remember, if the option Use Development Switches is 
enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment automatically sets switches 
to include all debugging information&per.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release Switches from the Target 
Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyRel:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog&per. 
 You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected 
in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the:hp2. CopyDev:ehp2. button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current 
dialog&per.  You can then make further changes, as required&per.  This button is available only if the Release switches are 
selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the 
Target Options submenu under the Targets menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. when you are finished setting switches for the selected source file or group of source files&per.
.br
.br
The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=50 id=Using_the_Autodepend_Switch.Using the Autodepend Switch
:i1.switches, autodepend
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, autodepend switch
.br
The Use Autodepend switch in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu keeps track of updated source files&per. 
 Once a source is modified, the IDE remembers that it needs to be recompiled the next time you do a make&per.  By using the 
Use Autodepend switch, the IDE also keeps track of changes made to source files on which other source files depend&per.
.br
.br
For example, let's say that your current project has a source file,:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source&per.cpp,:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
that depends on a header file,:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source&per.hpp&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  If 
the Use Autodepend feature is not enabled and changes are made to:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source&per.hpp:font facename=Courier size=0x0. 
after:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source&per.cpp:font facename=Courier size=0x0. in your current project has been compiled, 
the IDE does not recognize that your current source file is now out of date&per.  This means that the next time you do a 
make, the IDE will not compile:font facename=Courier size=12x10. source&per.cpp&per.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.  The 
Use Autodepend feature tells the IDE to compare the date and time stamps on all of the dependent source files in addition 
to the source files in the current project and their corresponding object files, so the IDE recognizes when your current 
source file needs to be recompiled&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To set the Use Autodepend switch&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu appears from the Target Options menu&per.  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select the Use Autodepend option:ehp2. from the Target Options submenu&per.
.br
.br
If the option was enabled when you selected it, you have disabled the Use Autodepend feature&per.  If the option 
had no check mark when you selected it, you have enabled the Use Autodepend feature&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=51 id=Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment.Configuring the Integrated Development Environment
:i1.customizing the IDE
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, customization
:i1.configuring the IDE
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, configuration
.br
The Integrated Development Environment can be reconfigured to some extent&per.  You can customize the following features&per.
:ul compact.
:li.Show or hide the Toolbar
:li.Show or hide the Status Bar
:li.Enable or disable automatic updates of file status in the target windows
:li.Select your own text editor
:li.Select a source revision control system
:eul.
.br
The next few sections describe these features&per.

:h3 res=52 id=Show_Toolbar.Show Toolbar
:i1.show tool bar
.br
The Integrated Development Environment Toolbar that appears under the Menu Bar can be removed from or added to the IDE 
window&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add or remove the toolbar&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose File:ehp2. from the menu bar&per.
.br
.br
A checkmark beside Show Tool Bar indicates that the toolbar is present on the screen&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select Show Tool Bar&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The Show Tool Bar state will be toggled&per.  If the toolbar was on the screen, it will disappear&per.  If the toolbar 
was not on the screen, it will reappear&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=53 id=Show_Status_Bar.Show Status Bar
:i1.show status bar
.br
The Integrated Development Environment Status Bar that appears at the bottom of the IDE window can be removed from or 
added to the screen&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add or remove the status bar&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose File:ehp2. from the menu bar&per.
.br
.br
A checkmark beside Show Status Bar indicates that the status bar is present on the screen&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select Show Status Bar&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The Show Status Bar state will be toggled&per.  If the status bar was on the screen, it will disappear&per.  If the 
status bar was not on the screen, it will reappear&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=54 id=Auto_Refresh.Auto Refresh
.br
The IDE will automatically update the target windows with the state of all files when this option is enabled&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To enable or disable auto refresh&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose File:ehp2. from the menu bar&per.
.br
.br
A checkmark beside Auto Refresh indicates that auto refresh is enabled&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select Auto Refresh&per.:ehp2.
.br
.br
The Auto Refresh state will be toggled&per.  When Auto Refresh is disabled, it may be necessary to select Refresh 
from the Window menu whenever the target window status becomes out of date&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=55 id=Selecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor.Selecting Your Own Text Editor
:i1.set text editor
.br
You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Integrated Development Environment&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To select your own text editor&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Set Text Editor:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
The Set Text Editor dialog appears&per.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field&per.  You must 
also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL)&per.  If the editor is an executable 
file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field&per.  The argument line will be supplied 
to the editor whenever it is started by the IDE&per.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will 
be filled in by the IDE&per.  The macros are&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.%f:ehp2.
:dd.The name of the file to be edited&per.
:dt.:hp2.%r:ehp2.
:dd.The row in the file at which to position the cursor&per.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains 
a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1&per.
:dt.:hp2.%c:ehp2.
:dd.The column in the file at which to position the cursor&per.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains 
a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1&per.
:edl.
.br
For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was&colon.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.      :font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     file&eq.'%f' row&eq.'%r' col&eq.'%c':font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the file:font facename=Courier size=12x10. 
foobar&per.c:font facename=Courier size=0x0. with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed 
to your editor is&colon.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.      :font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.     file&eq.'foobar&per.c' row&eq.'215' col&eq.'31':font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
.br
This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position 
the text cursor&per.  If no row or column is available, then the IDE will supply the value of 1 as a default&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select OK:ehp2. when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select Cancel:ehp2. when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor&per.
.br
.br
or
:dt.:hp2.:ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Select Default:ehp2. when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel&per.
:edl.

:h3 res=56 id=Selecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System.Selecting a Source Revision Control System
:i1.checkin source
:i1.checkout source
:i1.Perforce
:i1.Object Cycle
:i1.generic RCS
:i1.PVCS
:i1.MKS SI
:i1.MKS RCS
:i1.RCS
:i1.set source control
.br
You can use a source code Revision Control System (RCS) in conjunction with the Integrated Development Environment&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To select a revision control system:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Set Source Control:ehp2. from the File menu&per.
.br
.br
A number of options are presented&colon.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.MKS RCS:ehp2.
:dd.Mortice Kern Systems Revision Control System
:dt.:hp2.MKS SI:ehp2.
:dd.Mortice Kern Systems Source Integrity
:dt.:hp2.PVCS:ehp2.
:dd.Intersolv Project Version Control System
:dt.:hp2.Other:ehp2.
:dd.Any other flavour of revision control system (default)
:dt.:hp2.None:ehp2.
:dd.No revision control system
:dt.:hp2.Object Cycle:ehp2.
:dd.Powersoft's Object Cycle
:dt.:hp2.Perforce:ehp2.
:dd.Perforce
:edl.
:edl.
.br
When you select a source revision control system, the Checkout Source and Checkin Source items in the Sources menu are 
enabled&per.  The Checkout Source and Checkin Source items spawn batch/command files or dialogs corresponding to your selection 
of revision control system&per.  The batch/command files are located in the \WATCOM\BINW and \WATCOM\BINP directories&per. 
 These files can be modified to suit your environment&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.MKS RCS:ehp2.
:dd.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.MKS_CO:ehp2.
:dd.for checking out/locking files with MKS RCS
:dt.:hp2.MKS_CI:ehp2.
:dd.for checking in/unlocking files with MKS RCS
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.MKS SI:ehp2.
:dd.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Checkout Dialog:ehp2.
:dd.a check out dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI
:dt.:hp2.Checkin Dialog:ehp2.
:dd.a check in dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.PVCS:ehp2.
:dd.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.PVCS_CO:ehp2.
:dd.for checking out/locking files with Intersolv PVCS
:dt.:hp2.PVCS_CI:ehp2.
:dd.for checking in/unlocking files with Intersolv PVCS
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.Other:ehp2.
:dd.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.GEN_CO:ehp2.
:dd.a file which you can edit to provide check out (and lock) functionality to any generic revision control system
:dt.:hp2.GEN_CI:ehp2.
:dd.a file which you can edit to provide check in (and unlock) functionality to any generic revision control system
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.None:ehp2.
:dd.When selected, Checkout Source and Checkin Source are disabled&per.
:edl.
.br
When one of the batch files is spawned, a number of arguments are passed to the batch file&per.
.br
.br
For checking out files, the following arguments are passed&colon.
:ol.
:li."pause" or "nopause" to enable optional screen pause after completion of action
:li.full source file path specification
:li.full project file path specification (&per.wpj file)
:li.full target file path specification (&per.tgt file)
:eol.
.br
For checking in files, the following arguments are passed&colon.
:ol.
:li.full source file path specification
:li.full message file path specification (this file contains the textual message that you enter when checking in a file)
:li.full project file path specification (&per.wpj file)
:li.full target file path specification (&per.tgt file)
:eol.

:h2 res=57 id=Executing_Special_Commands.Executing Special Commands
:i1.after make
:i1.after
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, after make
:i1.before make
:i1.before
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, before make
.br
Sometimes you may find it necessary to execute commands before and/or after performing a make&per.  The Open Watcom Integrated 
Development Environment provides Before and After commands in the File and Targets menus&per.  These commands enable you 
to perform actions immediately before or after making either the selected target or all targets for the current project&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To set Before or After directives&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the target:ehp2. for which you want to set a before or after directive&per.
.br
.br
The title bar of the selected target is highlighted&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Target Options:ehp2. from the Targets menu&per.
.br
.br
A submenu appears from the Target Options menu&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Execute Before or Execute After:ehp2. from the Target Options submenu&per.
.br
.br
A Target Before or Target After dialog appears on your screen&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Note&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.If you want to set before or after directives to be executed when you make all targets, select the before and after commands 
from the File menu&per.
:edl.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the command:ehp2. you want to execute before or after the selected target is made&per.
.br
.br
:font facename=Courier size=12x10.:artwork align=center name='ide29.bmp'.:font facename=Courier size=0x0.
.br
:hp3.Figure 29&per.:ehp3.:hp3.:ehp3.:hp2. Enter execute commands in the Target Before or Target After dialogs&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click on OK:ehp2. to continue&per.
.br
.br
The IDE stores the information you entered and closes the dialog&per.  When you next perform a make on the selected 
target, the IDE will execute the before or after directive you entered&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=58 id=Adding_Resources_to_a_Target.Adding Resources to a Target
:i1.resources, adding
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, adding resources
.br
You may want to include resources in your target executable&per.  There are two ways of doing this in the IDE&colon. 
 indirectly and directly&per.  The act of adding resources to a target can only be done for targets that support resources, 
such as Windows or OS/2 PM&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add a resource to a target using the indirect method&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Create a new source file:ehp2. with the extension "&per.rc"&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the new source file:ehp2. to select it&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Edit Text:ehp2. from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Editor&per.
.br
.br
The editor opens on your screen with an empty window&per.  You can now add the resource file text&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Enter the resource statements:ehp2. in the editor&per.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Save your work and close the editor:ehp2. when you are finished&per.
.br
.br
You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  You can now compile the new source file&per.
:dt.:hp2.(6):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the new source file:ehp2. to select it&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file is highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(7):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Sources menu to compile the new source file&per.
.br
.br
The IDE compiles the selected source file&per.  To do this, the IDE invokes the Resource Compiler which creates a 
"&per.res" file&per.  The "&per.res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked&per.
:edl.
.br
When you create the resource file directly, the source file contains the same information but it is stored in a different 
format&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To add a resource to a target using the direct method&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Create a new source file:ehp2. with the extension "&per.res"&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the new source file:ehp2. to select it&per.
:dt.:hp2.(3):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Edit Resources:ehp2. from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Resource Editor&per.
.br
.br
The editor opens on your screen with an empty window&per.  You can now create the resources&per.
:dt.:hp2.(4):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Create the resources&per.:ehp2.
:dt.:hp2.(5):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Save your work and close the editor:ehp2. when you are finished&per.
.br
.br
You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window&per.  You can now compile the new source file&per.
:dt.:hp2.(6):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Click once on the new source file:ehp2. to select it&per.
.br
.br
The selected source file is highlighted in the target window&per.
:dt.:hp2.(7):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Choose Make:ehp2. from the Sources menu to compile the new source file&per.
.br
.br
The IDE compiles the selected source file&per.  The IDE does not need to invoke the Resource Compiler to create a 
"&per.res" file because you created the "&per.res" file directly using the Resource Editor&per.  The "&per.res" file will 
be automatically included in the target when it is linked&per.
:edl.

:h2 res=59 id=Including_Libraries_in_a_Target.Including Libraries in a Target
:i1.libraries, adding
:i1.Integrated Development Environment, adding libraries
.br
You use the same procedure to include static and DLL libraries in your target as you use to add any source file to a 
target&per.  The IDE includes the libraries when the target is linked&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.To create static and DLL libraries&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.
:dt.:hp2.(1):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Create a new target:ehp2. entering the library name in the Target Name field and choosing the appropriate image type&per.
.br
.br
Clicking on OK on the New Target dialog closes the dialog and creates a new target with the specified name in the 
title bar&per.
:dt.:hp2.(2):ehp2.
:dd.:hp2. Add the desired source files,:ehp2. new or existing, to the library you have created&per.
:dl break=all tsize=5.
:dt.:hp2.Note&colon.:ehp2.
:dd.If you want one of your other targets to use that library, you must enter the library as a source file for that target&per. 
 When you enter the library as a source file you must give it the extension "&per.lib", regardless of whether the library 
is a static or DLL library&per.  For example, if your library is entitled Target A and you want your existing Target B to 
use that library, add Target A as a source file on Target B with the extension "&per.lib"&per.
:edl.
:edl.

